    "topic_code": "3.7",
    "topic_title": "Sinusoidal Function Context and Data Modeling",
    "stem": "A marker on a vibrating machine moves vertically in a sinusoidal pattern between heights of 8 cm and 12 cm above a platform. It completes 3 full cycles each second and is at its maximum height at t = 0. Which function models its height h(t), in centimeters, after t seconds? Angles are measured in radians.",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "h(t) = 10 + 2cos(3t)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Frequency-as-parameter error: this uses 3 cycles per second directly as b. Because one cosine cycle spans 2π radians, b must be 2π · 3, not 3. Fix: Multiply the frequency by 2π before placing it inside cosine."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "h(t) = 10 + 2cos(6πt)",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The midline is (8 + 12)/2 = 10, and the amplitude is (12 − 8)/2 = 2. The frequency is 3 cycles per second, so b = 2π · 3 = 6π. Positive cosine starts at the maximum height, matching h(0) = 12."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "h(t) = 10 + 2cos((2π/3)t)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Frequency-period reversal: this treats 3 cycles per second as a period of 3 seconds. The actual period is 1/3 second, so b = 2π/(1/3) = 6π. Fix: Take the reciprocal of cycles per second to find seconds per cycle."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "h(t) = 10 + 4cos(6πt)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Full-range-as-amplitude error: this uses 12 − 8 = 4 as the amplitude instead of half that difference. The resulting heights range from 6 cm to 14 cm, contradicting the context. Fix: Divide the difference between maximum and minimum heights by 2 to find amplitude."
     }
    ]
   },
